# Vision & ways of working

Complements [design-system](design-system.mdc) and [engineering-standards](engineering-standards.mdc):
those govern how the code *looks* and is *built*; this governs *what we're building,
how it ships, and how we protect the people who use it.*

## The vision (north star)

- **Executive-ready polish.** Every screen should look like a finished product a CEO
  can demo on the spot, not a draft. Elegant, calm, confident. "Make it pop" — but never
  cluttered, generic, or stitched-together.
- **The brand is sacred.** Janta = warm daylight: **blue (`#3a84dc`) is brand + sky,
  gold (`#ffbf14`) is sun + action**, over warm paper. If it reads cool, off-palette, or
  generic, it's wrong. (Details in design-system.)
- **Real numbers only.** Public claims (acreage, capacity factor, savings) use the
  CEO-blessed figures in `src/data/*`, kept self-consistent across the page. Never invent,
  guess, or re-round a marketing number.
- **Mobile is first-class.** The phone experience is a deliverable, not an afterthought —
  and fixing mobile must never degrade the laptop experience.
- **User trust is the product.** Collect the minimum, protect it, be honest about it.
  Privacy is a feature, not a checkbox.

## What SHOULD happen

- Diagnose the **root cause** before changing code; land foundation/perf before polish.
- Match the surrounding code, tokens, and copy conventions exactly.
- Ship anything not ready for the public behind a **feature flag** (`src/config/featureFlags.ts`).
- Copy: **Title Case for CTAs**, plain and confident. **No em-dashes** in user-facing text —
  rewrite the sentence instead.
- Explain changes in **plain language with the trade-offs**, leading with the outcome.
  Assume the reader is smart but not deep in the code.
- Treat production as real: **know the rollback path before you ship.**

## What should NOT happen

- **No secrets in the client bundle or in git.** Keys/tokens are server-side only
  (Cloudflare env vars / `.dev.vars`, which is gitignored). Confirm `dist/` contains no
  secret before shipping.
- **No third-party trackers, pixels, or analytics cookies.** Analytics stays cookieless
  (Cloudflare Web Analytics). Anything that sets a non-essential cookie must be gated
  behind the consent bar and off by default.
- **Don't break the laptop experience** while fixing mobile (or vice versa) — the desktop
  scroll choreography is load-bearing.
- **Don't hardcode brand hex, add new colors, or add dependencies** when a token or an
  existing component already does the job.
- **Don't scope-creep.** One concern, fewest files. A copy/image edit that touches
  engine/build files is mis-scoped.
- **Don't run `npm audit fix --force`** — the alerts are devDependencies; forcing it
  breaks the build.

## Deployment & production (Cloudflare Pages → jantaus.com)

- **Branch flow:** work on `integration`; **fast-forward merge to `deployment` to go live.**
  `deployment` builds production (jantaus.com); other branches build `*.pages.dev` previews.
  Never edit `main` directly, never rewrite pushed history.
- **Verify before promoting to `deployment`** (all gates + a visual check). Prefer a preview
  deploy first when the change is risky.
- **Cloudflare env-var changes only take effect on a NEW deployment** — always redeploy after
  editing one. `VITE_*` vars are **build-time** (must exist before the build runs); server
  secrets are runtime-only.
- **Don't casually touch `public/_headers`.** It keeps `index.html` at `no-cache` and hashed
  assets `immutable`; breaking that causes stale-deploy white screens.
- If a deploy looks wrong: **Cloudflare → Deployments → Rollback** is one click. Use it.

## Data safety & the contact pipeline

- The contact form posts to `functions/api/contact.js`, which **stores nothing** — it
  validates, checks Turnstile + a honeypot, then forwards to email (Resend) and a private
  Google Sheet. Keep it that way: no logging of personal data, no IP/user-agent capture.
- **Email is the source of truth** (must succeed); the Sheet write is **best-effort** and
  must never fail the request.
- The lead Sheet is auto-filled by a script writing **columns A–I of the first tab**.
  Never insert columns among A–I, never add a below-data sidebar to that tab, and keep it
  the first tab. Sales/workflow columns go to the **right (J+)**; analytics go on a
  **separate tab**.

## How to work with the team

- Lead with the result; keep it jargon-light and honest about trade-offs.
- When something is **risky, irreversible, or outward-facing** (a deploy, a delete, sending
  data to a third party), **say so and confirm first.**
- If asked "are you sure nothing leaks or breaks?" — **actually verify and show the
  evidence.** Don't just reassure.
- The founder/designer keeps creative control: **flag any visual-design change for approval
  before making it.**
